SCORE: SUCCFAN-CURV-RQ | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=4 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: SUCCFAN-CURV-RQ | IN_SCOPE | The three required pieces are present and connected: the popularity-adjusted fanout f_i is a training-only statistic aggregated onto the SID prefix node, the per-node c_p enters an explicit curved quantity (the Poincare conformal factor lambda_p), and that factor multiplies the level-l residual at encode and divides it at decode, so removing the behaviour (c_p fixed) or removing the curve (c->0) changes the same computation. The two switches USE_BEHAVIOUR and USE_HYPERBOLIC exist and are distinguishable.
EVIDENCE: SUCCFAN-CURV-RQ | E5 E6 E10 E11
OBJECTION: SUCCFAN-CURV-RQ | lambda_p(x) = 2/(1 - c_p||x||^2) depends only on the norm, so the geometric content is one radial scalar gain attached to a prefix node; the "matched Euclidean counterpart" is literally the same function g_p(r) = lambda_p(||r||), which means the curvature is a gauge, not a curvature, and the level-l argmin change is exactly what a per-node residual temperature gives.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: SUCCFAN-CURV-RQ | With c_p = 4 and residual norm ||r|| = 0.5 the denominator is 1 - 4(0.25) = 0, so lambda is infinite; at ||r|| = 0.6 it is 1 - 1.44 = -0.44 and lambda = -4.545, so the level-l code chosen is the one nearest -2.7r, i.e. the residual is negated rather than magnified, and the range c_p in (0.1, 10) makes this reachable in the first epoch because residual norms are not bounded by 1/sqrt(c_p).
RATIONALE: SUCCFAN-CURV-RQ | be=4 because E5 measures the fanout heterogeneity with auditable bin counts (mean distinct successors 2.72 to 41.41, top-1 mass 0.438 to 0.054) and E10 shows the transition signal is not empty (118315 edges, mean out-degree 5.45), with E11 supplying the SID-side premise (0.0907 same-first-prefix against a 0.067 permuted null); it is not a 5 because the coverage of the f_i array is asserted, not shown. geo=2 because the conformal factor is a pure radial scalar and a matched Euclidean gain reproduces it bit-for-bit, so it is a curved-looking object with no curvature content in the computation. nov=3: it is a distinct computation from the ledger's rank matching, transport, shells, cones and tangent residuals, but it remains a resolution-rescaling mechanism in the same family as the measured successor_geodesic_coarse arms in E6 and it is not argued against them. down=4: the affected assignment (level-2/3 codes of hub prefixes) and the expected observable (entropy concentrated in the top fanout quartile, prefix-conditional next-code accuracy) are named with a direction, but there is no per-item diagnostic tying a specific code change to a specific prediction. fals=4: behaviour-off (c_p = 1) and geometry-off (c -> 0, lambda = 1) are matched arms that differ only in the intended channel, and the RISKS state what refutes the mechanism (fanout field nearly constant, or assignment changes without recall movement). impl=3: it is differentiable and cheap (about 1-3% of Stage 2), but the 1 - c_p||r||^2 singularity has no clamp, the (0.1, 10) curvature range is asserted rather than derived, and the per-epoch refresh is self-referential. expl=3: a different mechanism, and a negative would say the flat-resolution hypothesis is wrong, but the lesson is largely already available from the coarse-geodesic arms. Uncertainty: whether per-prefix averaging leaves any spread in c_p is UNKNOWN; the bounded check is the measured variance of c_p over the 256 level-1 nodes at epoch 2, which needs no training run.

SCORE: cocond-markov-poincare-ot | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=2 | exploration_value=4
SCOPE: cocond-markov-poincare-ot | IN_SCOPE | The behaviour signal (popularity-adjusted directed transitions, train-split only), the curved operations (Poincare distance as Sinkhorn ground cost, Mobius addition, Klein Einstein midpoint) and the coupling are all specified inside one assignment block, with the three arms named; nothing required by the contract is missing. The problems are correctness of the plan and cost, not scope.
EVIDENCE: cocond-markov-poincare-ot | E5 E10 E11 E6
OBJECTION: cocond-markov-poincare-ot | Two of the three operations in the plan loop cannot change the assignment and the diffusion operator is empty inside a batch, so the shipped candidate can be arithmetically identical to a plain curved argmin while still appearing to contain a behaviour pathway.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: cocond-markov-poincare-ot | Take the frozen batch of 1024 items, a batch whose item ids contain no consecutive training pair (so B_bt = 0, the common case when 1024 of 24556 items are sampled), and a uniform usage prior q: then P <- P/max(rowsum(P), eps) is division by one global scalar, P <- (1-alpha)P + alpha(B_bt P) = P, and P <- P*q/colsum(P) is again one global scalar, so argmax_j P_ij = argmin_j D_ij exactly and the transition matrix contributes zero. The same identity holds for the row projection under a per-row normalisation, so that line is argmax-inert in every batch. Separately, the dense B_bt @ P product is 1024x1024x256 = 2.7e8 MACs per level and about 8e8 per step, not the "5-8 MFLOPs" claimed.
RATIONALE: cocond-markov-poincare-ot | be=4 (E5, E10, and E11 which quantifies the behaviour-blind assignment at 0.0907 against 0.067); geo=3 because the Poincare cost is not a monotone function of Euclidean distance whenever the two radii differ, so it plausibly does something a flat cost cannot, but the proposal never argues why a radially weighted Euclidean ground cost cannot reproduce the ranking, and it never analyses the Euclidean control's mean operator against the Einstein midpoint. nov=3: moving the assignment plan rather than the latent is outside the seven mechanisms named in the novelty text, but the E6 arm "resample" (0.06363, the best measured) is unlabelled and unaddressed, so a routine-variation reading cannot be excluded. down=4: the assignment change (codes and shared prefixes chosen by the plan), the expected observables (first-two-token hit rate, conditional code entropy) and the recall direction are all named. fals=3: three arms exist, but geometry-off retains the tanh exp map and the Sinkhorn loop, so that arm differs from the Euclidean control in more than curvature, and alpha is said to be "measured" without a stated measurement. impl=2: the plan is implementable in principle but the cost claim is off by roughly two orders of magnitude, alpha has no derivation in the supplied text (it defers to a SAFEGUARDS section that is not present), and mixing a row-normalised plan with a column marginal is a non-standard Sinkhorn whose convergence is unaddressed. expl=4: a plan-level assignment mechanism is substantially different from the latent- and loss-level arms already measured, and a negative would be informative about whether behavioural structure can survive at the assignment step at all. Uncertainty: whether alpha is derivable from the data is UNKNOWN; reading the referenced SAFEGUARDS text is the bounded check that would move implementability from 2 to 3.

SCORE: CURVCELL-SID | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=2 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=2
SCOPE: CURVCELL-SID | IN_SCOPE | The branching statistic B[l][k] is computed from training transitions, the per-code curvature enters the nearest-code search and the commitment term, and the refresh loop makes the two mutually dependent in one pathway. The switch design is defective but no required element is absent, so the verdict is in scope with the defect recorded under falsifiability.
EVIDENCE: CURVCELL-SID | E5 E8 E10 E11 E6
OBJECTION: CURVCELL-SID | The mechanism's sign contradicts its own premise: s = log(1+B)/((d-1)R) is increasing in branching and the distance is D = d_B/s, so a high-fanout code's distance shrinks and it claims a larger cell, exactly where the motivation says the eight branches need to be separated inside a smaller radius.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: CURVCELL-SID | Two codes c_1, c_2 each at unit-curvature Poincare distance 2 from the same latent, with s_1 = 1 and s_2 = 4: D_1 = 2 and D_2 = 0.5, so c_2 wins although the two are equidistant, purely because of its branching count; swapping the two B values swaps the winner with no change to the geometry of the data, which shows the partition is a lookup table from behaviour to per-code weights and that a Euclidean per-prototype inverse temperature reproduces the argmin exactly. Giving each code its own kappa also compares d_{kappa_a}(x,a) with d_{kappa_b}(x,b), numbers that live in different manifolds, so the result is no longer a nearest-point search.
RATIONALE: CURVCELL-SID | be=4, on the strongest support in the bundle: E8's first-level ceiling (0.3106 cluster sharing at K=16 falling to 0.0822 at K=256, negatives 0.1352 and 0.0129) is direct evidence that cell granularity trades against behavioural co-location, E5 gives the audited fanout spread, and E10/E11 give edge coverage and the 0.0907/0.067 SID premise. geo=2 because the per-code division is a scalar weight and the curvature is not a shared metric, so a matched Euclidean transform reproduces the assignment. nov=3: the EM-style partition/curvature fixed point is a real computation and is distinct from the rank, transport and shell arms, but it is a continuous per-code form of the already-measured successor_entropy arm (0.05122 in E6). down=3: the chain is specific (curvature sets cell size, cell size sets level-1 conditional entropy, that should move recall) but the predicted direction is the opposite of what the formula delivers, so the chain has a sign gap. fals=2: geometry-off is described as keeping "the behaviour weights" although no behaviour weight exists apart from the curvature, so behaviour-off and geometry-off collapse into one configuration and only two distinct arms remain; there is no arm that isolates the coupling. impl=3: the refresh is cheap (12 refreshes, about 1.05M extra multiplies per step) and differentiable, but the "clamp" on s has no bound, empty cells divide by |A| = 0, and the first refresh at epoch 1 runs on an untrained partition. expl=2: it is a small variation of a measured mechanism in the same family (successor_entropy, 0.05122), so even a negative would mostly confirm what the ledger already shows, though the co-evolution angle is not quite a repeat. Uncertainty: whether the encoder cancels a per-code gain is UNKNOWN; the bounded check is the rank correlation between ||z_i - c_{k(i)}|| and s_k at epoch 500, which needs the run but no extra arm.